@extends('layouts.demo')

@section('title')
  Layouts and sections
@endsection

@section('heading')
  Layouts and sections
@endsection

@section('sidebar')
  @parent
  <span>+ one link from this page</span>
@endsection

@section('note')
  This page fills the note section, so the note appears.
@endsection

@section('body')
  <p><code>@@extends</code> and <code>@@section</code> make a page use a layout, and <code>@@yield</code> marks where each section appears. The layout on this page is itself a layout: it extends the app layout, and this page extends it. Propel follows the chain from the page up to the app.</p>

  <h2><code>@@yield</code> with a default</h2>
  <p>A default prints when no section fills the name. The heading above, the lead text and the sidebar all come from that.</p>
<pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-blade">&lt;title&gt;@@yield('title', 'Propel')&lt;/title&gt;</code></pre>
  <p>The lead text below is the default from the demo layout, because this page does not fill the lead section.</p>

  <h2><code>@@parent</code></h2>
  <p>Inside a section, <code>@@parent</code> prints what the same section contains one level up. The sidebar on this page adds to the demo layout's default links, instead of replacing them.</p>
<pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-blade">@@section('sidebar')
  @@parent
  &lt;span&gt;Extra link from this page&lt;/span&gt;
@@endsection</code></pre>

  <h2><code>@@show</code></h2>
  <p>In a layout, <code>@@section</code> with <code>@@show</code> defines a section and renders it where it stands. A page that fills the section replaces the default, and <code>@@parent</code> keeps the default inside the page's version. Rendering the layout alone shows the default.</p>
<pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-blade">@@section('sidebar')
  Default links
@@show</code></pre>

  <h2><code>@@hasSection</code> and <code>@@sectionMissing</code></h2>
  <p>Check whether a page filled a section. The note on this page appears because the page fills the note section.</p>
<pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-blade">@@hasSection('note')
  &lt;p&gt;@@yield('note')&lt;/p&gt;
@@endif</code></pre>
  <p><code>@@sectionMissing</code> is the opposite. It renders when the page did not fill the section.</p>
<pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-blade">@@sectionMissing('note')
  &lt;p&gt;This page has no note.&lt;/p&gt;
@@endif</code></pre>

  <h2><code>@@include</code></h2>
  <p>Renders another view in place. The partial sees the same data as the page, and the loop variables of the loop it is in.</p>
<pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-blade">@@include('partials.badge')</code></pre>
  <h3>Live example</h3>
  @include('partials.badge')

  <h2><code>@@push</code>, <code>@@prepend</code> and <code>@@stack</code></h2>
  <p>A page pushes content into a named stack, and the layout prints the stack. Scripts are the usual case. Pushes from the page are printed even when the layout renders them before the page's own content. <code>@@prepend</code> puts content at the start of the stack.</p>
<pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-blade">@@push('scripts')
  &lt;script src="/chart.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
@@endpush</code></pre>
  <p>The layout prints the stack with <code>@@stack</code>:</p>
<pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-blade">&lt;footer&gt;@@stack('scripts')&lt;/footer&gt;</code></pre>
  <p>A page can also prepend:</p>
<pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-blade">@@prepend('scripts')
  &lt;script src="/first.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;
@@endprepend</code></pre>
  <h3>Live example</h3>
  <p>The footer of this demo layout prints the script this page pushed. It is a text footer here, so the script does not run.</p>

  @push('scripts') <code>pushed from this page</code> @endpush

  <h2>Views from a package: <code>name::view</code></h2>
  <p>A package can ship its own views and layout. It registers them under a name, and the app refers to them with that name and two colons, as Laravel does with <code>loadViewsFrom</code>. Ignite does this for its admin panel.</p>
<pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-rust">propel_rocket::register_namespace("ignite", "vendor/ignite/views");</code></pre>
  <p>Then a view can extend the package's layout:</p>
<pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-blade">@@extends('ignite::layouts.app')

@@section('content')
  &lt;p&gt;The panel's content.&lt;/p&gt;
@@endsection</code></pre>
  <p>A name with no namespace still reads from <code>resources/views/</code>. The part before <code>::</code> may only contain letters, digits, <code>_</code> and <code>-</code>, and the view part follows the same rules as any name. A namespace that was never registered is a render error, and the error page names it.</p>
@endsection
